the last year or so has taken a real  toll on my mental health  as i'm sure it has for lots of people  and if you're subscribed to this channel  or you're watching this video chances  are you've turned to games  for some comfort it's clear that games  can have a positive  effect on our mental health but does it  matter which games we're playing  personally speaking since the pandemic  began i've spent hundreds of hours  playing  securo bloodborne the dark souls series  and demon souls and it's given me plenty  of comfort but  i don't really know why what is it about  these  intense bleak games that i found so  soothing  when i could have been playing something  like animal crossing or minecraft  now as someone who's only got a very  basic understanding of psychology that's  a difficult question for me to answer  so i got in touch with alexander chris  he's a psychotherapist  and author of the gaming mind a book  that looks at why we play  games and the positive impact that games  can have on those who play them  i've played games since i was very young  child it was late in my training when i  started to  become aware of how many young people  were presenting to the clinics and  hospitals where i was working  where gaming was a big part of their  presentation a big part of their lives  so i started to research and write  more about gaming from the perspective  of a  mental health professional of a  psychologist though the gaming mind was  written prior to the pandemic the timing  couldn't have been better  video games have remained a growth  industry over the last year with studies  reporting that 62  of adults played some form of video game  in 2020.  more people are playing games than ever  before and with that comes the potential  for people to develop their own  positive relationships to games so does  it matter what they're playing  you know there were a few games like  animal crossing being a very obvious one  that  had such a serendipitous launch it was  sort of almost  like exactly what would be called for at  the onset of a global pandemic  i think depending on the individual and  what they're dealing with  you know i've seen people take such  solace in it i've also known  patients in the past year who picked up  animal crossing and found it  so anxiety provoking like the last thing  they wanted to do was like pay back a  loan  from my perspective it sort of starts  with the person and then  moves on from there to the game games  create this opportunity  for people to kind of explore themselves  and their relationships  and what that actually means for a  person is very particular not only to  the game but to  them right their own psychology so what  exactly is it that i am getting out of  playing the souls games i mean  at this point we should probably address  the elephant in the room which is  they're [ __ ] hard it's 12 years since  the release of the original demon souls  and in that time the game and its  successors have had a profound  impact on video games leading to a  nascent genre of souls likes and a  cottage industry of challenge run  streamers and  husky voiced lore diving youtubers  invariably these games contain grand  bleak worlds opaque stories  and nightmarish creatures to battle they  are complex and strange games  and yet for the most part if you ask  someone what they think of when they  think of a souls game  it's that they are difficult  in his book alexander discusses a  patient who finds comfort in playing  fallout 4  on the punishingly difficult survivor  mode so i asked him if any research has  been conducted into the possible  benefits  of playing hard games as that's my  knowledge not been specifically  researched  but um you know is is worthy of it  especially given  the at this point now enduring  popularity of games that are regarded as  brutally difficult  there is a real validation of  the defeat right that is part of the  experience games definitely lend  themselves to  experiences of mastery and it can  certainly bring a lot of satisfaction  and people are feeling frustrated or  confined in their  lives especially because you know you  can accomplish things in games that are  a lot easier in games than in real life  you can swing a sword you can  slam dunk a basketball right things that  most people can't  do i think for a lot of people  those kinds of experiences can really be  a kind of compromise right between  the desire to feel competent and  accomplished  and like you've really done something  that not everyone could do  but also having to deal with the very  real frustrations that can exist in  people's  lives outside of games so what does all  this mean exactly  basically that in a time when we're all  feeling such a profound loss of control  over the direction of our lives it's  perfectly natural that we would turn to  video games to regain it and  particularly games that offer  such a sense of control and achievement  games like dark souls also  you get to swing a massive old big bit  of wood around  so there's that on a more personal note  i see playing these games as a kind of  mindfulness exercise  if you're not familiar mindfulness is a  kind of meditation which involves  bringing your attention to the present  moment rather than ruminating on things  in the past  or the future traditionally it involves  more familiar forms of meditation  like focusing on breathing or the  sensations of your immediate  surroundings  as someone who's had treatment for  depression and anxiety in the past this  traditional kind of mindfulness  has been recommended to me on more than  one occasion but i am  dreadful at it i am an anxious  distracted person and  i've tried long walks i've tried deep  breathing yoga  meditation being mindful but  my mind is always racing and some of  these things work  not always consistently though but one  thing over the past year that i have  noticed does work for me is playing the  souls games  because of their difficulty these games  tend to require a high level of  attention  in most cases you can't even pause the  game in a way i think they function as a  kind of  attention trap for me it's hard to sit  there worrying about the state of the  world when there's a boss the size of a  house  running at you it's hard to feel  distracted or have the desire to look at  your phone when you're working through a  game environment that is so  actively hostile because of all this i  think it quiets the part of my brain  that is usually filling my thoughts with  worries  now i am very aware that not everyone  will get this from playing these games  and i want to be clear that  while this works for me i'm in no way  suggesting that playing these games  is a blanket cure for anyone else's  depression  or anxiety in fact a lot of you probably  have  the opposite relationship to these games  and if that's the case you might have  more  in common with alexander than you do  with me  i played bloodborne briefly and  um didn't get very far but other than  that the only souls game i ever played  was demon souls and i was playing it in  the context  of a transition that was happening in my  professional life i was feeling guilty  about  some of the choices i was making in my  professional life i was leaving  in particular one patient behind i found  it to be this  incredibly maddening experience but i  played it a lot  during that period for a long time so  for me  how i came to understand that in  retrospect was that actually at that  time in my life i was looking for  something  uh kind of masochistic by the time i was  done with demon souls i was almost like  done with video games for a while like i  just was so fed up  i think that in some ways is so  illustrative of games right the two  people  playing the same game having totally  diverse and psychological experiences  that really speak to  who they are and what they're dealing  with as someone who plays these games  it's hard to deny that it feels great to  take down a boss you know many people  are gonna struggle with the series seems  to elicit the sense of communal pride  and accomplishment that you rarely see  in other games perhaps because every  player has been through the same  highs and lows there's a sense of a  community working through challenges  together  following the discovery of a locked door  in the ps5 remake of demon souls  thousands tuned in to watch distortion 2  become the first person in the world  to open it it's a series that lets you  share in other people's gutting losses  and glorious victories whether it's  other flunking a no hit run on boss  number 41 of 41  or three idiot friends beating dark  souls  on a transatlantic flight  so yeah they're difficult games and it  feels good when you beat them but  i don't think that's all there is to it  despite how difficult and intense they  are  the soulsborne games is something that  people turn to as a kind of  comfort to help them cope with mental  health difficulties  and i think despite how bleak  and oppressive the games are  a big part of that is to do with their  tone  to some extent the souls games feel like  games for our time  they meet you on your level everything  in the game is bleak everyone is sad  hopeless given the situation we've all  found ourselves in the last year  is pretty resonant now i absolutely love  mario games for example but when i'm  feeling down  i don't always want mario grinning at me  i want a character  a situation a world that reflects how  i'm feeling back at me  much in the same way that it's sometimes  cathartic to listen to sad music when  you're feeling sad  this melancholia extends to the  non-playable characters who tend to fall  into archetypes across the series  a crestfallen warrior a clumsy oaf  unable to live up to his idea of heroism  a determined but ultimately doomed  adventurer  they are invariably people who have felt  the impact of this world  someone that speaks to a loss  anxiousness and depression  in a way that can reflect your mood in  doing so they become a measuring stick  for your growth  as you progress through the game we  unkindled are worthless  can't even die right at the core of  understanding oneself is the process  of symbolizing what we go through with  language right and putting words to  feelings the crestfallen warrior or the  sort of archetype right  is saying what at least some aspect of  the player is thinking  there's no salvation here you'd have  done better to rot in the undead asylum  there can be something very relieving  in hearing one's experience reflected  concisely with words right and and in  that way feeling less alone  even though this is not another human  player it's an npc  just um hearing that somebody else  agrees on some level like  this is never going to work out hearing  it voiced and expressed then gives it  a name and a face right and it's  something that you can work with  gives the player permission to be  feeling hopeless  as opposed to suggesting that the player  should be  progressing well and feeling really good  about it and  uh if they have doubts that they'll make  it to the end that's their problem  but we're talking true legends  with the metal to link the fire we're  not fit to lick their boots  now i'm well aware that a lot of what  i've talked about in this video could  sound a bit like pseudoscience but  thinking about this stuff can have a  real impact on people's lives  part of alexander's goal through his  work is to help his patients  take the self-esteem benefits that they  get from playing games  and apply them to their day-to-day lives  outside of games  put simply one of the things that makes  it hard for someone to kind of transfer  their skills and self-esteem from a game  into  the rest of their life is that we're  conditioned to believe that  games are not relevant and important to  the rest of life so as a psychotherapist  you know my  the primary kind of technique that i use  we can call it that  is that if a patient plays games or  mentions games i just take them  seriously  right i'm curious about it like i would  be about anything else they might bring  into a session and it's uh it still  doesn't cease to surprise me  how novel that typically is for the  individual  i don't think there's anything to fear  in coming to a deeper appreciation of  our relationship with games i really  think we only have  benefits right only only gains can come  from  being more open and curious about why we  love games so much  despite how hard they are and despite  the bleak worlds they present i can  totally see why playing these games is a  kind of comfort blanket  they are repeatable learnable rhythmic  they are  unchanging and fixed in a time when so  much has been uncertain  they offer an opportunity to work  towards satisfying and rewarding goals  and might hold up a kind of mirror to  how you're feeling  they offer the chance to feel like part  of a community and sharing other  people's triumphs  and disasters they are everything we  love about video games  now you may hate the souls games and  that is absolutely fair they are clearly  not for everyone and if you've made it  this far into the video and that is the  case then  thank you so much for watching this has  clearly been a very personal video for  me to make  but hopefully something of what i've  said has resonated with you too  maybe there is a game or a series that  has given you some comfort over the past  12 months or  so i hope that is the case let me know  in the comments  down below if you have enjoyed this  video then subscribe so we can make more  content like this and maybe drop us a  like too  and in the meantime be safe don't go  hollow  but there is something greatly  comforting about that flame  it seems to fulfill something very  precious  deep within the soul something  [Music]  essential  [Music]  you

